<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saronic Gulf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Aegina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saronic Gulf is an arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> peninsula of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, bounded to the west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>. Its islands include Salamis, Aegina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poro">Poros</a>, and its shores held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>, Megara and Troezen. In 480 BCE the Greek fleet defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in the straits of Salamis at its northern end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saronic-gulf.jpg" alt="Saronic Gulf" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Panagiotis Botsis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Argo-saronic_EN.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
